class=3D"mb-(--prose-body-margin-bottom,--spacing(6)) last:mb-0">We next tu=
rn to the average number of =E2=80=9Cagent workdays=E2=80=9D per researcher=
 per day, where we define every eight hours of agent use as one agent workd=
ay. In contrast to agentic spend, agent workdays better capture the amount =
of =E2=80=9Cwall clock time=E2=80=9D agents spend working, but do not accou=
nt for changes in model capabilities or pricing.</p></div>
<div class=3D"col-span-full toc-visible:@md:col-start-2 @md:col-span-6 @md:=
col-start-4 max-w-none min-w-0 in-[:where(li)]:contents not-first:mt-6"><p =
class=3D"mb-(--prose-body-margin-bottom,--spacing(6)) last:mb-0">Overall, a=
gent workdays per researcher have risen from 0.5 agent workdays per researc=
her to 5.7 agent workdays per researcher, an elevenfold increase, with the =
largest increase in teams focused on pretraining and posttraining. On those=
 teams, agent runtime averaged roughly eight workdays per researcher per ca=
lendar day. In scaling and infrastructure, the average was roughly four. We=
 note that this workday metric generally includes interactive use only, so =
it may undercount automations.</p></div>
