class=3D"mb-(--prose-body-margin-bottom,--spacing(6)) last:mb-0">Alongside =
these trends by estimated duration, we also examine how success varies acro=
ss the different types of tasks researchers might choose to delegate. We re=
port estimated success rates by category and task, using the <a href=3D"htt=
ps://epoch.ai/gradient-updates/toward-an-onet-for-ai-rnd" class=3D"transiti=
on ease-curve-a duration-250 text-primary-100 hover:text-primary-60 underli=
ne-offset-[0.25rem] decoration-1 underline relative" target=3D"_blank" rel=
=3D"noopener noreferrer" data-analytics=3D"mdx-body-link">O*NET AI R&amp;D =
taxonomy<!-- -->=E2=81=A0<span class=3D"sr-only">(opens in a new window)</s=
pan></a> developed by Epoch AI. Unsurprisingly, our results suggest that mo=
dels are not equally good across all task categories: for instance, from Au=
gust 1, 2026 through October 18, 2026, estimated success rates without huma=
n intervention was higher for Communicate (69%) and Analyze (58%) than for =
Build (43%). Similarly at the task level, estimated success rates were high=
er for tasks such as monitoring runs for issues (79%) and answering technic=
al questions (72%), whereas they were lower for writing code for inference =
and serving stacks (25%) and building evaluation, reinforcement-learning, a=
nd agent environments (29%). So long as model performance on AI R&amp;D-rel=
ated tasks is heterogeneous, lower-performing categories and tasks could be=
come bottlenecks to high overall uplift.</p></div>
